  "rationale": "The subject `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-calls.ContextPhase` declares a public unit struct, `pub struct ContextPhase;`, and describes its role as a marker. Its only testable obligation is that this exact public type exists, with that declaration. How the phases behave is specified and tested by other requirements, such as `agent_context` and `judge.phases`, and those are not reassessed here.\n\nThe {test} reference is the `:test:` anchor on the requirement. It points to `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]`.\n\n- **Build and inventory:** The test runs `cargo doc` for the crate. `specified_exports` parses the public API inventory, and `md/requirements/sdk/public-api.md` lists `struct ContextPhase` there with a declaration link to this requirement.\n- **Export set:** `documented_exports` walks the rustdoc item tables and collects each export's path and kind. The final assertion requires no missing and no unexpected exports, so `ContextPhase` must exist as a public `struct` at the crate root.\n- **Declaration:** `check_declaration` reads the requirement's ```rust,ignore block, `pub struct ContextPhase;`. It compares its normalized signature parts with the published rustdoc declaration, and fails on differences in either direction. It also asserts that the trait impl set and member names match.\n- **Description:** It checks that the requirement has a non-empty description.\n\nTogether these assertions catch the plausible violations. A removed or renamed struct fails the export diff. Changing it to an enum, giving it fields or making it private fails the kind or declaration comparison. Adding unexpected trait impls fails the impl-set equality.\n\nThe sentence \"marks an agent request that can still accept context\" describes the type's role rather than a separate behavior of the struct. Its use is covered by the requirements that cite it, including `Ask<…, ContextPhase>::context`. The test is correctly marked with a {test} reference. I found no gap, and there are no {fixme} acknowledgments.",
